WEBVTT

00:00:00.680 --> 00:00:02.840
You rarely have just one perfect

00:00:02.719 --> 00:00:05.200
semantic model. You have dozens.

00:00:05.560 --> 00:00:07.200
That's true. Marketing has a semantic

00:00:07.080 --> 00:00:09.600
model, sales has a semantic model,

00:00:09.740 --> 00:00:11.399
finance has one, and they all

00:00:11.280 --> 00:00:13.020
have a concept called customer.

00:00:13.300 --> 00:00:14.859
Yes, the classic overlap.

00:00:15.280 --> 00:00:16.380
If I use the agent to

00:00:16.260 --> 00:00:17.680
ingest all three of these models

00:00:17.560 --> 00:00:19.800
into one master ontology, how does

00:00:19.680 --> 00:00:21.180
it reconcile the fact that marketing

00:00:21.060 --> 00:00:23.140
defines a customer as anyone who

00:00:23.020 --> 00:00:24.300
gave us an email address?

00:00:24.980 --> 00:00:26.520
Well, finance defines a customer as

00:00:26.400 --> 00:00:28.280
someone who has actively paid an

00:00:28.160 --> 00:00:29.500
invoice in the last 90 days.

00:00:29.720 --> 00:00:32.059
That is exactly where the automated

00:00:31.939 --> 00:00:34.339
generation process stops and human

00:00:34.220 --> 00:00:35.919
governance must take over.

00:00:36.100 --> 00:00:37.680
So the AI doesn't just fix

00:00:37.560 --> 00:00:40.680
it? No. The ontology Agent is

00:00:40.560 --> 00:00:41.559
a powerful assistant.

00:00:41.740 --> 00:00:42.980
It will help you bring multiple

00:00:42.860 --> 00:00:44.980
semantic models into a shared space,

00:00:45.320 --> 00:00:46.779
and it will flag those overlapping

00:00:46.660 --> 00:00:49.220
concepts. It will propose ways to

00:00:49.100 --> 00:00:50.980
reconcile them, but it is not

00:00:50.860 --> 00:00:52.800
an arbiter of corporate policy.

00:00:53.640 --> 00:00:55.600
A human domain expert still has

00:00:55.480 --> 00:00:57.440
to sit down, review the proposed

00:00:57.320 --> 00:00:59.580
entity keys, check the relationships,

00:01:00.220 --> 00:01:02.279
explicitly decide how to handle that

00:01:02.160 --> 00:01:05.000
naming collision, usually by utilizing

00:01:04.879 --> 00:01:07.960
namespaces or creating distinct marketing

00:01:07.840 --> 00:01:10.200
customer and finance customer entities.
